## Trying the mod in Claude Code

- One session: `claude --plugin-dir {% raw %}/absolute/path/to/{% endraw %}{{ project.slug }}`.
- Every session: add the folder to `env` in `~/.claude/settings.json` and restart Claude Code:

  ```json
  { "env": { "CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS": "/absolute/path/to/{{ project.slug }}", "CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_FUNCTION_HOOKS": "1" } }
  ```

  `CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_FUNCTION_HOOKS` is only needed on Claude Code versions that still gate function hooks. To switch the mod off, remove the folder from the list (or use `/{{ project.slug }} off`).
- Several mods may be loaded at once and each owns its own status line text; if two fight over `$.ui.status`, combine them in one module.
- Mods run with the owner's permissions and are not sandboxed. Do not add network calls, shell commands or file access the mod's purpose does not need, and say so in the README when it does.

## How to add…

**A setting.** (1) `hooks/lib/config.ts`: the field in `ModConfig`, its default, handling in `resolveConfig` and `parsePatch`. (2) `ui/src/settingsSchema.ts`: a field definition (label and help through `t()`); if it needs a new control, add the kind in `components/Settings.vue`. (3) `ui/src/i18n/ru.ts`: the Russian text of every new `t()` string. (4) Use it in the hooks. (5) Tests in `tests/config.test.ts`.

**A hook.** In `hooks/register.ts` add `on('<event>', async ($, e, next) => …)`. Always `return next(e)` (or the result of it) unless the handler's job is to answer. Put the logic in `hooks/lib/` as pure functions with their own tests; `register.ts` only calls `$`. List events and their payloads in the `plugin-authoring` skill and `.claude/types/claude-code.d.ts`.

**A slash command.** The command is the plugin name; sub-commands are words in `runCommand`. Add the word, its message in `hooks/lib/messages.ts` (English **and** Russian; `tests/messages.test.ts` checks both), update `usage`.

**An API route.** `ui/lib/handler.ts` (validate input with a function from `hooks/lib/`, answer errors through `tr()` so they follow `X-Mod-Lang`), a method in `ui/src/api.ts`, a test in `tests/handler.test.ts`.

**Styling.** {% if choices.css == "tailwind" %}`ui/src/style.css` defines the page's building blocks as component classes (`.card`, `.btn`, `.input`, `.badge`…) with `@apply`; the components use these class names and no utility classes. Add a block there, then use it. Light and dark are handled by `dark:` variants.{% else %}`ui/src/style.css` is one plain stylesheet: colours are CSS variables in `:root` (with dark values under `prefers-color-scheme: dark`) and the building blocks are classes (`.card`, `.btn`, `.input`, `.badge`…). Add a class there, then use it; no CSS framework is installed.{% endif %}

**A new page or tab.** `ui/src/components/<Name>.vue` and mount it in `App.vue`. Add a router only when there are several pages.

## Rules and pitfalls

- **The hooks environment is not Node.** `$.env.get(...)`, `$.fs.read(...)` and the other engine calls take **literal** names/arguments the validator can list; a computed name fails `npm run validate`. Do not import Node modules or anything outside `hooks/` from `register.ts` or `hooks/lib/`.
- **`hooks/lib/` is pure** (no `$`, no Node, no DOM): it is shared by the engine, Node and the browser and unit-tested directly.
- **A broken module does not load at all, in any session.** Run `npm run validate` (or `check`) before you say a change works; after edits the running session hot-reloads the module, so a type error shows up immediately.
- **Hooks must never throw into the session.** Catch, log with `$.ui.log`, and fall back to "do nothing".
- **Settings UI is local only**: the server binds to `127.0.0.1`, checks `Host` and `Origin` and requires the `X-Mod-Request` header on every change. Keep it that way; never add a route that reads files or runs commands from a request.
- **`.env` is read only by the server, Vite and scripts**, with `util.parseEnv` (exact values, no `$VAR` expansion). The hooks never read it. `APP_NAME` is the page title; the plugin's `name` (the slug) is the identity.
- **Renaming the mod** means changing the plugin `name` (it moves the data folder and the command); migrate the old data folder if the mod was already used.
- **Languages.** UI strings are English in code, `t('Text')`, with Russian in `ui/src/i18n/ru.ts` (`tests/i18n.test.ts` enforces it). The mod's own messages are in `hooks/lib/messages.ts`. Keep the code, comments, docs and commit messages in English.
- Dependencies are pinned to exact versions in `package.json`. Add one only when clearly needed. The settings server must stay dependency-free; TypeScript stays on 5.9 until `vue-tsc` supports newer versions.

## Testing

- `tests/*.test.ts` run in Node. Pure modules are tested directly; `tests/handler.test.ts` starts the real handler on a free port; `tests/register.sim.test.ts` runs the real `register.ts` against a fake engine (`$` with an in-memory file system and a scripted process runner) — extend its fake when you use another `$` call.
- The page has no component tests by default; its translations are tested (`tests/i18n.test.ts`). Look at it in a browser (`npm run dev`) after changing it, in both languages and in light and dark mode.
- Check with a real Claude Code before you finish a behaviour change: load the mod (see above) and watch it.

## Definition of done

`npm run check` passes with zero warnings; the behaviour was tried in Claude Code; `README.md` and this file are updated when commands, settings or structure changed.

